meta {
  name: Stream (SSE, skipped in runs)
  type: http
  seq: 13
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/notifications/stream
  body: none
  auth: bearer
}

headers {
  Accept: text/event-stream
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{notifAdminAccessToken}}
}

script:pre-request {
  // A Server-Sent Events response is held open until the token's `exp` — up
  // to fifteen minutes. The CLI runner would sit on it for all of that time,
  // so during a collection run this request documents the endpoint and skips
  // itself. Sending it by hand from the Bruno app still works (`skipRequest`
  // is a no-op outside a run): the frames appear as they arrive, and Cancel
  // ends it. To watch it from a shell instead:
  //   curl -N -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" -H "Accept: text/event-stream" \
  //     http://localhost:4000/api/v1/notifications/stream
  bru.runner.skipRequest();
}

docs {
  # GET /notifications/stream — the live feed (PAC-154 PR2)

  One Server-Sent Events connection per tab. Authenticated with the same
  Bearer header as everything else (the web client uses a `fetch`-based
  EventSource for that; a token in the URL would land in every proxy log).
  **No permission**, `@SkipTenant() @SkipBranch() @SkipModule()` — a
  notification is addressed to you. **Every throttler is skipped** (a
  reconnect wave after a deploy must not 429 the badge), so the only limit is
  **8 open streams per user per API node**: the ninth is a `429` the client
  backs off from.

  ## Frames

  | event | when | data |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | `ready` | at once, with `retry: 3000` | empty — the client refetches the count + list on every open |
  | `ping` | every 25 s | empty — keeps the balancer / proxy idle timeouts from closing the socket |
  | `notification` | when the worker writes a row for the caller | the stored `NotificationRecord` (`id:` is the row id) |

  The stream **ends by itself one second before the token's `exp`** (≤ 15
  min; a token with no `exp` gets 15 min flat) so a stream never outlives its
  token; the client refreshes the token *before* reconnecting when it is
  within 30 s of expiry, and reconnects at once after a clean close. A node
  shutting down ends every stream it holds.

  The row is re-read **once per recipient per nudge** and handed to every tab
  of that user; a nudge for a row that is not the caller's is dropped. A read
  that fails drops that one frame — never the stream, and never the driver's
  error text.

  ## Responses

  - `200 text/event-stream` with `Cache-Control: no-cache, no-transform`,
    `X-Accel-Buffering: no`, `Connection: keep-alive`.
  - `401` — no / expired token (reconnect after a refresh).
  - `429` — this user already holds 8 streams on this node.

  ## Not run by the CLI

  `bru.runner.skipRequest()` in the pre-request script: the runner cannot
  consume an event stream and would hang on it. Covered end to end by
  `packages/api/test/notifications-stream.e2e-spec.ts` (ready frame + headers,
  nudge → frame, one read per nudge across tabs, foreign nudge dropped, the
  cap, close at `exp`).
}
